joining me is the former permanent  special representative for climate  change that's sir david king so david  good to have you with us this morning  now of course with your experience um i  guess what we're seeing right now in the  uk was predicted but it's worse than we  thought maybe  it is worse than we expected okay and  it's all coming sooner than we expected  i think we now  do have an understanding of these  extreme weather events  they're occurring of course right across  europe it's not just britain  suffering both from drought and from  extreme temperatures  and it's i believe driven by what's  happening in the arctic circle region  where ice has been  melting much more rapidly than was  predicted and now during the three polar  summer months which is right now  much of the arctic ocean is exposed to  sunlight as before it was covered with  ice so it was reflected back into space  so that heating of the arctic circle  region  is the cause of what we're looking at  the  reason  can be explained in some detail but let  me just say  we have  now got to anticipate this kind of  weather going into the future and if  anything getting worse  the the combination of a drought and the  risk of fires with the  heat wave  is is very severe  and i i would say to people  yes it's lovely to have a nice hot day  to get out in the sun  but please don't spend much time in the  sun heat stress is awful if you're in  the sun your your local temperature is  well above 35 36 degrees centigrade and  your body temperature should not exceed  37  so getting rid of that excess heat  becomes a real problem  and if you're sweating in other words  it's not your sweat isn't evaporating it  means your sweat isn't acting in the way  it should do to cool your body down you  need to be in a cool breeze in the  sunshine or you need to have a fan on  you so that you're blowing the sweat  away and as it evaporates it cools your  body down  i said david are there enough measures  being put in place uh  by governments to try and help us deal  with the heat waves that we're going to  be facing as you rightly point out for  the foreseeable future  i think what we need is a coordinated  plan  from government to manage  to adapt our societies and our position  against these challenges and no we don't  have this plan in place  it is a plan that is vitally necessary  and  it would be very very important to see  that  the the government when it gets over  this election business  and  it is frightening that we're in some  state of stasis at the moment without  actions coming through from the very top  um but the the plan needs to begin to be  put in place so that next summer  we are actually in in a state of  preparedness and there is a bullet for  the incoming government to to bite and  that is it is public services that are  needed to be improved whether it's  hospital staff health care staff  fire services all of these  have been severely underfunded over the  last decade and we need to see urgently  a refunding of our public services  well apart from an increase in funding  which of course many people will um  want and appreciate that that is  necessary what else can the government  be doing at this stage  well at this stage it's it's really a  bit too late the government needs to be  putting out warnings and i hear you  doing that very well the the fires are  the the biggest risk we're faced with  and this is uh an extreme problem of  course the other problem is that faced  with a drought we have water shortages  and we all need to now be learning to  treat water as a precious commodity so i  think the the problems are coupled  together shortage of water drought fires  and high temperatures  much more needs to be done  in order to bring us to a much more  secure place  and sir david have you hearing anything  from the current um contenders to lead  the conservative party that gives you  encouragement that they take the crisis  we're facing seriously  well frankly no  and that that is a matter of concern it  would be so important to  have the the questions asked of any  incoming prime minister what actions  will you take to reduce the impacts of  climate change and frankly there's been  no discussion of this at all which is  extremely disappointing and i would say  also extremely worrying  now  there's been talk of course of of  removing those green levees just to  describe for us um what you think that  would do they say they're going to do it  temporarily to try and ease the pain  that some households are facing but what  do you think that would do what kind of  message does it send if we start going  back on our targets to reduce our carbon  emissions okay your question implies the  answer doesn't it in the sense that  quite clearly green levees are one way  of us preparing for uh climate change we  we need to see  that for example  in the face of the current crisis  driven by the  war in in europe around ukraine  we we are seeing  energy prices rise very very rapidly now  the right reaction is not  to invest in fossil fuels to see if we  can bring the price down by putting more  fossil fuels into the marketplace that  would take far too long we've got a  crisis that's here now how do we manage  it today and the way to manage it is to  increase energy efficiency to ask people  to  really look after  their use of energy sources  air conditioning and so on we need to  see that shops do keep their doors  closed uh when they've got air  conditioning on we need to see a whole  series of measures to save energy going  forward in time and these measures are  being taken in places like italy and  france  and of course we should be doing exactly  the same  all of us across europe are faced with  the similar crises very very similar and  we need to take actions right across the  board  now those measures of course to try and  make sure that homes are better prepared  to deal not just with the heat but also  with the cold that 